President Herbert Hoover wrote this letter in 1931 to 10-year-old Barbara McIntyre of Columbus, Ohio, after she wrote to him explaining that she and her friends planned to collect old blankets, clothing, shoes, and food to send to Washington for distribution to the poor.

@date The White House Washington November 19, 1931

@dear My dear Barbara,

I have your very sweet letter of November 10th. It is a beautiful undertaking. I would suggest, however, that instead of sending the contributions which you collect to me, that you should distribute them to those in need in your own locality.

@close Yours faithfully, // Herbert Hoover

President Franklin D. Roosevelt sent this letter to clergymen around the country.

@date The White House, Washington September 24, 1935

@dear Reverend and Dear Sir:

Because of the grave responsibilities of my office, I am turning to representative clergymen for counsel and advice—feeling confident that no group can give more accurate or unbiased views. I am particularly anxious that the new Social Security legislation just enacted, for which we have worked so long, providing for old age pensions, aid for crippled children, and unemployment insurance, shall be carried out in keeping with the high purposes with which this program was enacted.

I shall deem it a favor if you will write to me about conditions in your community. Tell me where you feel our government can better serve our people. We can solve our many problems, but no one man or single group can do it—we shall have to work together for the common end of better spiritual and material conditions for the American people.

@close Very sincerely yours, // Franklin D. Roosevelt
